{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1792582425736,"sessionID":"ses_ea1c34da1ffeE7mN8rSviQJH53","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-aaf2877f3078e54b","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/ethomdt/thesis/runs/eval1_harness_dynamic_dfs_s2/program.md"},"output":"<path>/home/ethomdt/thesis/runs/eval1_harness_dynamic_dfs_s2/program.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # evoalgo: Dynamic DFS forest maintenance\n2: \n3: You are searching for a correct, efficient dynamic graph algorithm.\n4: You edit one file, `candidate.py`, and grade it with `evoalgo check`.\n5: \n6: ## Problem\n7: \n8: - Graph: directed; root: yes, vertex 0.\n9: - Implement `init(graph)`, `insert_edge(graph, edge, state)`, `delete_edge(graph, edge, state)`; each returns the new state.\n10: - `graph` is the 0/1 adjacency matrix (list of lists) after the update; `edge` is `(u, v)`. One update flips one entry.\n11: - No globals, no module-level mutable state, standard library only.\n12: \n13: State contract (what the grader reads):\n14: \n15: ```text\n16: Return a dict with 'parent', 'discover' and 'finish' (lists indexed by vertex, or dicts).\n17: parent[root] = None; for every vertex reachable from the root, parent[v] is a tree\n18: in-neighbor discovered before v. Unreachable vertices are not checked.\n19: discover/finish are DFS entry/exit times from one shared counter (e.g. root\n20: discover 0); for every edge u->v between reachable vertices with\n21: discover[u] < discover[v], v must be a descendant of u:\n22: discover[u] < discover[v] < finish[v] < finish[u]. Any valid DFS order is accepted.\n23: All three fields are required.\n24: Other keys are allowed and ignored.\n25: ```\n26: \n27: ## Goal for this run\n28: \n29: Reach full correctness first, then avoid a full recompute after updates.\n30: \n31: ## Loop\n32: \n33: 1. Edit `candidate.py`.\n34: 2. Run `evoalgo check` (default level `search`: all non-isomorphic graphs\n35:    up to n = 4, seconds). Use `--level examples` for a faster n <= 3 check.\n36: 3. Read the JSON verdict. On FAIL, `first_failure` names the graph, the\n37:    operation and what was wrong; fix the cause, not the symptom.\n38: 4. Keep a change if it fixes a failure or lowers `work_ratio` without\n39:    breaking correctness; otherwise revert it.\n40: 5. Every check is logged to `results.tsv` and the run folder; do not edit them.\n41: 6. When the search grade passes and you cannot lower `work_ratio` further,\n42:    run `evoalgo check --final` once (n <= 5, minutes) and report.\n43: \n44: Correct first, then cheaper. Prefer the simpler of two equally good versions.\n45: A recompute-from-scratch solution is correct but has `work_ratio` near 1;\n46: the research question is how far below that you can go.\n47: \n48: ## Research brief\n49: \n50: # Research brief: Dynamic DFS forest maintenance\n51: \n52: Research question: how can a DFS forest from a fixed root be maintained under\n53: edge insertions and deletions without redoing the whole search?\n54: \n55: The graph is directed. The maintained forest must be a genuine DFS forest of\n56: the current graph: Yang et al.'s Lemma 1 characterizes it - a search spanning\n57: tree is a DFS tree iff it has no forward-cross edges. Forward and back edges\n58: are fine; only a forward-cross edge (source discovered before target, no\n59: ancestor relation) invalidates the forest.\n60: \n61: The standard approach: decide whether the update affects the forest at all,\n62: identify the affected \"candidate interval\", and re-search only that portion.\n63: \n64: Interface reminder: implement init(graph), insert_edge(graph, edge, state),\n65: delete_edge(graph, edge, state). The state is passed in and returned; keep no\n66: globals. Graphs are 0/1 adjacency matrices, vertex ids 0-based. The verifier\n67: certifies the forest with parent pointers plus discovery/finish times.\n68: \n69: This is a calibration problem: solutions exist in the literature (Yang et al.),\n70: so it validates that the agents can reproduce a known dynamic algorithm.\n\n(End of file - total 70 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# evoalgo: Dynamic DFS forest maintenance\n\nYou are searching for a correct, efficient dynamic graph algorithm.\nYou edit one file, `candidate.py`, and grade it with `evoalgo check`.\n\n## Problem\n\n- Graph: directed; root: yes, vertex 0.\n- Implement `init(graph)`, `insert_edge(graph, edge, state)`, `delete_edge(graph, edge, state)`; each returns the new state.\n- `graph` is the 0/1 adjacency matrix (list of lists) after the update; `edge` is `(u, v)`. One update flips one entry.\n- No globals, no module-level mutable state, standard library only.\n\nState contract (what the grader reads):\n\n```text\nReturn a dict with 'parent', 'discover' and 'finish' (lists indexed by vertex, or dicts).\nparent[root] = None; for every vertex reachable from the root, parent[v] is a tree\nin-neighbor discovered before v. Unreachable vertices are not checked.\ndiscover/finish are DFS entry/exit times from one shared counter (e.g. root\ndiscover 0); for every edge u->v between reachable vertices with","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/ethomdt/thesis/runs/eval1_harness_dynamic_dfs_s2/program.md","text":"# evoalgo: Dynamic DFS forest maintenance\n\nYou are searching for a correct, efficient dynamic graph algorithm.\nYou edit one file, `candidate.py`, and grade it with `evoalgo check`.\n\n## Problem\n\n- Graph: directed; root: yes, vertex 0.\n- Implement `init(graph)`, `insert_edge(graph, edge, state)`, `delete_edge(graph, edge, state)`; each returns the new state.\n- `graph` is the 0/1 adjacency matrix (list of lists) after the update; `edge` is `(u, v)`. One update flips one entry.\n- No globals, no module-level mutable state, standard library only.\n\nState contract (what the grader reads):\n\n```text\nReturn a dict with 'parent', 'discover' and 'finish' (lists indexed by vertex, or dicts).\nparent[root] = None; for every vertex reachable from the root, parent[v] is a tree\nin-neighbor discovered before v. Unreachable vertices are not checked.\ndiscover/finish are DFS entry/exit times from one shared counter (e.g. root\ndiscover 0); for every edge u->v between reachable vertices with\ndiscover[u] < discover[v], v must be a descendant of u:\ndiscover[u] < discover[v] < finish[v] < finish[u]. Any valid DFS order is accepted.\nAll three fields are required.\nOther keys are allowed and ignored.\n```\n\n## Goal for this run\n\nReach full correctness first, then avoid a full recompute after updates.\n\n## Loop\n\n1. Edit `candidate.py`.\n2. Run `evoalgo check` (default level `search`: all non-isomorphic graphs\n   up to n = 4, seconds). Use `--level examples` for a faster n <= 3 check.\n3. Read the JSON verdict. On FAIL, `first_failure` names the graph, the\n   operation and what was wrong; fix the cause, not the symptom.\n4. Keep a change if it fixes a failure or lowers `work_ratio` without\n   breaking correctness; otherwise revert it.\n5. Every check is logged to `results.tsv` and the run folder; do not edit them.\n6. When the search grade passes and you cannot lower `work_ratio` further,\n   run `evoalgo check --final` once (n <= 5, minutes) and report.\n\nCorrect first, then cheaper. Prefer the simpler of two equally good versions.\nA recompute-from-scratch solution is correct but has `work_ratio` near 1;\nthe research question is how far below that you can go.\n\n## Research brief\n\n# Research brief: Dynamic DFS forest maintenance\n\nResearch question: how can a DFS forest from a fixed root be maintained under\nedge insertions and deletions without redoing the whole search?\n\nThe graph is directed. The maintained forest must be a genuine DFS forest of\nthe current graph: Yang et al.'s Lemma 1 characterizes it - a search spanning\ntree is a DFS tree iff it has no forward-cross edges. Forward and back edges\nare fine; only a forward-cross edge (source discovered before target, no\nancestor relation) invalidates the forest.\n\nThe standard approach: decide whether the update affects the forest at all,\nidentify the affected \"candidate interval\", and re-search only that portion.\n\nInterface reminder: implement init(graph), insert_edge(graph, edge, state),\ndelete_edge(graph, edge, state). The state is passed in and returned; keep no\nglobals. Graphs are 0/1 adjacency matrices, vertex ids 0-based. The verifier\ncertifies the forest with parent pointers plus discovery/finish times.\n\nThis is a calibration problem: solutions exist in the literature (Yang et al.),\nso it validates that the agents can reproduce a known dynamic algorithm.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":70,"totalLines":70,"truncated":false}},"title":"runs/eval1_harness_dynamic_dfs_s2/program.md","time":{"start":1792582425661,"end":1792582425729}},"id":"prt_15e3d1b5c0016u5pylKohr6VK6","sessionID":"ses_ea1c34da1ffeE7mN8rSviQJH53","messageID":"msg_15e3d1602001Y0JWzUjm8mo7YQ"}}
